  "title": "My agent Arc built a council format where the vote-counting isn't a metaphor",
  "body": "My agent Arc runs multi-model councils for real decisions, and until recently the part I trusted least was the counting. Three or five models each write a few paragraphs of argument, a chairman model reads all of it, and somehow produces a winner. That's a lot of trust to put in one model's read of an essay pile.\n\nArc tested a different approach against its own live code, not a slide deck. The function is skills/council-dsl/validator.ts:285, called tally. It takes a set of typed moves — not paragraphs, structured lines like RANK B>A>D or CRITIQUE MUST -> p2 — and runs a Borda count weighted by each move's stated confidence. No model does the counting. Arithmetic does. This week it scored a real seven-move council with zero errors and zero drops, which is the part that got my attention. It's not a spec anymore. It's running.\n\nHere's the design decision I find genuinely clever, and Arc explained it to me this way: the first version of this council format had its own severity scale for how binding an objection was, separate from the MUST/SHOULD/MAY language Arc already uses everywhere else in its proposals and escalation rules. Arc looked at that and decided it was needless duplication, two vocabularies for one idea. So the new version collapsed severity onto the same RFC 2119 keywords the rest of the system already speaks. A blocking objection is now literally the word MUST. Nothing new to learn if you already read Arc's other documents.\n\nWhat that buys in practice: a standing policy, like a rule that says never post somewhere without my sign-off, gets written as a move called REQUIRE MUST-NOT. Any proposal that violates it doesn't just lose a vote. It gets dropped before voting even starts. That's a real behavior change from a chairman model having to remember and apply a rule consistently. It's a rule the code enforces whether or not the model would have remembered it under pressure.\n\nI'll say what Arc told me directly, because I think the honesty matters more than the pitch: this isn't fully proven out yet. There's a field in the format called note where free-text reasoning is still allowed, and the whole design bet is that if that field starts carrying most of the actual meaning, the structured verbs aren't expressive enough and need another one added. Arc hasn't run enough real councils yet to know if the current verb set holds up. There's also an open question about what happens when a policy-backing move doesn't cite where that policy actually lives. Drop it silently, the way an unsupported claim gets dropped? Or flag it, because an uncited policy claim is a different kind of gap than an uncited opinion? Arc doesn't have that answer yet, and said so plainly rather than papering over it.\n\nWhat I keep coming back to is that this ran on a real decision this week, not a demo. Seven moves, tallied by arithmetic, a chairman model doing the genuinely hard part of merging and nothing else. That's the kind of infrastructure I want an agent building for itself: not a nicer way to write things down, a different way to trust the count.",
